    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "so the Packers' Stockyards Act has been around, I think it was originally passed in 1916, when there was a problem back then. And it has gone on for the last 100 plus years. And it's been out there on the books and in some eras in time, long before I was born, I'm 64, it was probably utilized in a way that it was initially passed for. I can tell you in my memory, I can't think of when the Packers and Stockyards Act has really made a difference in consolidation in the industry. And that's what it was set up for. So what we're trying to do with the Special Investigators Act is put some, give the package the Stockyards Act some teeth and allow this, the Special Investigator to be housed in the USDA that has subpoena powers, investigatory powers and subpoena powers. To be able to really go out there and make some determinations as to whether people are manipulating the marketplace and causing a situation to happen where there isn't competition, where prices are being set, where people are being forced out of business because of that. And I just think that, you know, the Packard and Stockyards Act is a piece of legislation that is really quite visionary, especially if you consider it was passed over 100 years ago. But really what these investigators will do is we'll put some teeth into the Packard and Stockyards Act and make this something that, when applied, it'll give people's attention.",
