  {
    "episode_id": 74989,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74989.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "So I can tell you that almost everywhere I go in the state, and you know, agriculture is our number one industry in Montana, but almost everywhere I go, people are coming up to me and saying things like, geez, we appreciate what you're doing because Margins are so thin right now, we can't pair bills. And I hear that regularly. I hear regularly from people that say, and I don't know if these figures are right, I have in fact check them, but they'll say, how come the packers are making $1,300 a head, and I can't afford to buy hay, okay? I heard from a gentleman that's been generational in the livestock business, And this was about a year ago, he said, if Congress doesn't do something, and this guy is conservative, his conservative could be. And he said to me, if Congress doesn't do something here, our food chain is gonna change, and it isn't gonna be for the better. And when it changes, we'll never get it back to where we had it. So food predictability will be something that we're gonna have a hard time make ensuring that that's there for our citizens. Look, I hear a lot. I do. From folks in production agriculture, cow-calf operators, from small and medium-sized feeders, that the system is rigged, and it's been rigged for a long time, and that we need to not take over the marketplace, but just ensure the marketplace works. And that's the whole impetus behind the Special Investigator Act.",
