  {
    "episode_id": 74989,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74989.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Well, all I want, quite frankly, is to be competition in the marketplace. So you've got people that are competing for the product that's out there. And to be honest with you, I mean, I'll be straight up with you guys. I don't know that it can be done in the current system. So that's why, and I don't mean reworking it. I mean, let's get some more players into it. Let's empower some of the small and medium-sized folks to at least take up a piece of this pie of meat production in this country. So that we have a small meat processor, say, in Montana that has the ability to sell meat across state lines. That's going to allow him to expand his plant or her plant and hire more people. And it'll give a market for those producers in southwestern Montana that wouldn't be there normally that might want to fat a few cattle. And I think that's really the bottom line is that more competition in the marketplace is my goal. That's what I want to see happen. I want to see capitalism work. I want to see folks in the cow cap business and the small, medium sized feeders, actually there's competition for the product they raise because they put a lot of investment in it. And I think the United States raises the best products in the world. And quite frankly, they ought to get paid a fair price for it. I don't think you can continue to have a system like we have now. without significant federal subsidies. And that's not a direction I want to go. I want to have this competition so that the federal government is removed from the marketplace, not an inserted into it. In the meantime, you know, there has to be some teeth put in the Packers and soccer attack to make sure that that competition exists.",
