  },
  {
    "episode_id": 74989,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74989.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "So I've been through several administrations since I was first sworn in as U.S. Senator, and this is the first administration that's really taken the bull by the horns and addressed the issue of egg consolidation and competitive markets. The president just had an executive order that went out last week, I believe, to address anti-competition under the Packers and Stockyards Act. I think that that's a great first step I do think there's several bills out there that can help the effectiveness of what the president wants to do. One of them is a special investigator, another one is a bill that I've got to get cash prices up to 50%. There's no magic in 50%. Maybe it's 40%, maybe it's 30%, but the cash prices are way too low right now and that helps manipulate formula pricing and that can cause manipulation to drive the prices down. So those two bills I'd like to see get done. And I think we need to continue to work with our allies in the WTO to give mandatory country of origin label, and I think that would help a lot. Also, as far as what's going on in this country, as far as consumer choice, and prices at the farm gate. So I appreciate what the president did with his executive order. I appreciate what Secretary of Hill Sachs doing with setting aside $500 billion for small plants to either start up or expand in the processing business. I think it's something we need throughout this country. So I would say, kudos to the president. I think he's taken steps in the right direction. I think Congress still needs to do their job.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
